{#
	One widget's card, drawn on the request that fetched it (XIV-66).

	This is the whole of the generic mount: the dashboard put a widget key on the
	page, `loading="defer"` made the browser come back for the contents, and this
	renders whatever that widget's panel names. Everything about *what* a panel is
	stays in the widget; everything about *when* it arrives stays here.

	`panel.values` is where the widget's promise is resolved, and this is the only
	place it is called on this path — which is what makes the deferral save the
	queries rather than merely move them (see `Xivi\Core\Dashboard\WidgetPanel`).

	`only`, like the inline branch on the dashboard itself: a panel is rendered
	with exactly what its widget handed over and cannot quietly start depending on
	a variable that happens to be in scope. The globals every template has —
	`chrome`, `app` — are unaffected.

	A null panel draws an empty card rather than throwing. A widget can stop
	applying between the page rendering and this request arriving, and a module
	somebody uninstalled in another tab is not a reason for a 500.
#}
<div{{ attributes }}>
	{% set panel = this.panel %}

	{% if panel is not null %}
		{{ include(panel.template, panel.values, with_context = false) }}
	{% endif %}
</div>
